entry mass-defect
term: Mass Defect
difficulty: advanced
image: ../../assets/images/terms/Mass Defect.webp
related_terms: binding-energy
related_terms: nuclear-fission
related_terms: nuclear-fusion
related_terms: chain-reaction
quick_facts: Nucleus weighs less than its separated parts
quick_facts: Missing mass converted to binding energy via E = mc²
quick_facts: Helium-4: ~0.7% mass loss = ~28 MeV
quick_facts: Measured with mass spectrometers of extraordinary precision
quick_facts: Predicts stability — and which reactions release energy

sources:
    {"organization":"HyperPhysics","title":"Mass Defect","url":"http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/NucEne/nucbin.html"}

definition:
    The measured shortfall between a nucleus's actual mass and the added-up masses of its free protons and neutrons. That missing mass didn't vanish — it became binding energy via E = mc² when the nucleus formed. A helium-4 nucleus is about 0.7% lighter than its parts, worth roughly 28 MeV. The bigger the defect per nucleon, the tighter the nucleus is bound and the more stable it is — which is exactly why fission and fusion release energy as they climb toward iron.

debunking_snippet:
    “Mass is always conserved.” Not quite — mass-energy is. Every nuclear reaction that powers stars, reactors, and bombs trades a sliver of mass for enormous energy.
